<!-- Delivery Semantics pilot. Exact approved source revision and scope are in source-snapshot.json. -->
<!-- @opening -->
<p class="story-lead">The consumer commits order M42. Its acknowledgement disappears. The sender times out and sends M42 again. A second delivery has happened; a second order does not have to.</p>

Follow the message, the committed effect and the sender's knowledge separately. This chapter's retry loop cannot remove uncertainty on its own. The receiver's replay contract decides whether uncertainty becomes a duplicate effect.

<!-- @before Historical Context :: guide -->
### Name the boundary before naming the guarantee

A transport acknowledgement, broker acceptance and completed business operation confirm different things. [RabbitMQ distinguishes publisher confirms from consumer acknowledgements](https://www.rabbitmq.com/docs/confirms); neither alone is an end-to-end business receipt. Also, UDP itself can duplicate datagrams: “send once” is an application policy, not duplicate suppression by UDP ([RFC 8085 §3.3](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc8085.html#section-3.3)).

<!-- @before First Principles :: animation -->
