The picture's database boundary matters: the consumer's external API calls would sit outside it. The full text sequence remains available with JavaScript disabled.

<!-- @before Internal Mechanics :: guide -->
### What the capture transaction must own

The Kotlin blocks below are application-shaped fragments: they assume the shown app APIs, a transport adapter and, where used, coroutine/time imports. They are not standalone programs. The consumer requires a real database implementation. Its database adapter must serialize duplicate scoped keys and retry serialization conflicts as appropriate. Only writes through that same transaction are captured. A pre-transaction lookup is an optimization, not the concurrency guard ([PostgreSQL isolation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html)).

For an external payment or email, commit an outbox intent locally, then use downstream idempotency and reconciliation. The outbox relay can deliver twice; a local commit does not atomically charge a provider ([transactional outbox](https://github.com/aws-samples/transactional-outbox-pattern)).

<!-- @before Tradeoffs :: guide -->
### Read the comparison under its failure assumptions

“No data loss” assumes durable retention, retries and eventual recovery, within the system's stated failure envelope. A finite attempt limit, message expiry, cancellation or unhandled storage loss can end the attempt without confirming success. Latency rankings depend on batching, durability and acknowledgement policy; deduplication does not prescribe a round trip per record ([Kafka delivery semantics](https://kafka.apache.org/41/design/design/)).

<!-- @after Failure Modes :: staff -->
### Keep the identity through uncertainty

For M42, retain the original account, immutable payload and operation key. If the replay record expires before a late retry, reconcile authoritative state rather than mint a new key and risk another order. Key retention and duplicate-effect protection must be agreed together ([Stripe API v1 replay contract](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests)).

<!-- @after Answer Key :: guide -->
### Scope the original answers

As you read the answers, treat answers 1–3 and 8 as claims about a specified boundary: general unreliable communication leaves uncertainty, while coordinated systems can provide scoped guarantees. Kafka's transaction covers its output and input offsets; external effects need destination cooperation ([Kafka design](https://kafka.apache.org/41/design/design/)).

For answer 4, persisting the key is necessary but also requires backend account/payload binding, atomic capture and retention. For answer 7, a late key needs reconciliation when retry lifetime cannot be bounded ([Stripe idempotency](https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests)). For answer 10, distinguish a durable notification record from a push hint: provider acceptance does not prove device delivery or display ([FCM message lifetime](https://firebase.google.com/docs/cloud-messaging/customize-messages/setting-message-lifespan)).
